  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts DNA behavior in normal solution with behavior in a eutectic water–ice phase. In solution, changing conditions toward higher temperature or lower pH promotes strand separation, while the reverse favors simple reannealing of the two complementary strands. This is shown as a basic reversible melting and re-forming process between the red and blue strands.\n\nIn the ice-containing eutectic phase, the process appears different and more useful for assembly. Cooling and raising pH favor polymerization, converting short duplex segments into longer double-stranded products labeled with a higher degree symbol. A green enzyme-like shape in the center suggests that a catalyst mediates this ligation or extension. The figure emphasizes that this polymerization is “more efficient” in the eutectic phase than in ordinary solution.\n\nA key mechanism highlighted on the right is triplet-dependent inhibition of reannealing. Small short fragments bind in a way that seems to interfere with the strands simply zipping back together, reducing unproductive reannealing and thereby favoring polymer growth instead. Overall, the message is that freezing into a eutectic ice phase shifts nucleic acid strands from ordinary reversible melting toward more efficient, template-guided polymerization.",
